---
import DocsShell from '~/components/DocsShell.astro'
import Preview from '~/components/Preview.astro'
import PropsTable from '~/components/PropsTable.astro'
import DataTableDemo from '~/demos/data-table-demo'

const featuresExample = `import {
  rowSortingFeature,
  createSortedRowModel,
  sortFn_alphanumeric,
  tableFeatures,
} from '@tanstack/react-table'
import type { StackColumnMeta } from '@bwmp-dev/ui/table'

// Only what this table uses, so nothing else is bundled.
export const features = tableFeatures({
  rowSortingFeature,
  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),
  sortFns: { alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric },
  columnMeta: {} as StackColumnMeta,
})`

const serverExample = `const table = useTable({
  features: defaultTableFeatures,
  columns,
  data: query.data?.items ?? [],
  manualSorting: true,
  manualPagination: true,
  rowCount: query.data?.total ?? 0,
  state: { sorting, pagination },
  onSortingChange: setSorting,
})`
---

<DocsShell
  title="DataTable"
  description="Styling, sorting affordances and states over a TanStack Table instance you own."
>
  <p>
    <code>DataTable</code> deliberately does not create the table. You call <code>useTable</code>
    yourself, so every TanStack capability — faceting, grouping, virtualisation, manual server-side state
    — stays directly reachable. What the component provides is the markup, the sorting affordances, selection
    styling, the loading skeleton and the empty state.
  </p>
  <p>
    It lives behind <code>@bwmp-dev/ui/table</code> because <code>@tanstack/react-table</code> is an optional
    peer dependency. Nothing in the main entry point imports it.
  </p>

  <Preview
    demo="data-table-demo"
    title="Sorting, selection, filtering and pagination"
    contained={false}
  >
    <DataTableDemo client:visible />
  </Preview>

  <h2>Features are opt-in</h2>
  <p>
    TanStack Table v9 only ships the features you register. <code>defaultTableFeatures</code> covers what
    a typical dashboard table needs, but a table that only sorts should compose a smaller set and keep
    the rest out of the bundle:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{featuresExample}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>DataTable</code> detects what the instance actually registered, so sorting, selection and column
    visibility are all optional.
  </p>

  <h2>Server-driven tables</h2>
  <p>
    When the server does the sorting and paging, tell the table so and hand it the page it should
    render. Nothing about the component changes:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{serverExample}</code></pre>
  <p>
    In the application template, that state lives in the URL, so a sorted, filtered, paged view is
    linkable and survives a reload.
  </p>

  <h2>Column meta</h2>
  <p>
    <code>DataTable</code> reads three display hints from <code>columnDef.meta</code>:
    <code>align</code>, <code>className</code> and <code>hideBelowMd</code>. They are typed, because
    <code>defaultTableFeatures</code> registers <code>StackColumnMeta</code> in its
    <code>columnMeta</code> slot.
  </p>

  <h2>Selection</h2>
  <p>
    <code>selectionColumn()</code> is a plain column definition rather than a prop, so it sits in your
    <code>columns</code> array where you can reorder, pin or omit it. Pass it through the column helper:
    <code>helper.display(selectionColumn())</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>API</h3>
  <PropsTable type="DataTableProps" />
  <PropsTable type="DataTablePaginationProps" />
</DocsShell>
